{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rtk read /Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-init/SKILL.md /Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-conventions/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: docs-init\ndescription: Set up project memory in a repository. Creates docs/index.md and docs/architecture/stack.md from the templates, writes the project-memory block into the root AGENTS.md between its markers, and makes sure CLAUDE.md imports AGENTS.md. Use when a user asks to initialize, set up or add project memory, the docs layer or the spec workflow to a repo, or runs docs-init. A rerun refreshes only the block.\n---\n\n# docs-init\n\nSets up the docs layer once and refreshes the block on a rerun. It never maps the product: `behavior/`, `glossary.md` and `decisions/` grow through docs-map and the spec commands.\n\n## Process\n\n1. Load docs-conventions. Its `references/index.md`, `references/architecture.md` and `references/agents-block.md` hold the rules for the three files this command writes, and its `assets/` hold their templates.\n2. Find the docs path. If the root AGENTS.md already has a project-memory block, read the path from it. Otherwise it is `docs`, unless `docs/` already serves a documentation site, which a `mkdocs.yml`, a Docusaurus or Sphinx config, or an `index.html` under it gives away. Then ask for a subfolder and recommend `docs/project`.\n3. Decide first run or rerun. If `<docs-path>/index.md` exists, this is a rerun: skip to step 7 and touch no docs file.\n4. Look before asking. Read the README and the package or build files for the product name and the install, run, test, lint and build commands. List any `.specify/`, `specs/`, `openspec/` or `.kiro/specs/` folder, any AGENTS.md below the root, and whether a root CLAUDE.md exists. None of this is asked.\n5. Ask what only the user knows, in one numbered round with a recommended answer each: what the product is in 2 to 4 sentences, when the README doesn't say; the first domain and its owner, the user on a solo project; the issue tracker's address, if the team has one; and a confirmation of the detected commands. If the request already gives these facts, don't ask.\n6. Write the two files from their templates, every guidance comment removed, no empty section: `<docs-path>/index.md` with what it is, the Domains table, and `issue-tracker` only when given; `<docs-path>/architecture/stack.md` with the Commands table and only the rows that have a command. Create no other file and no empty folder.\n7. Write the block into the root AGENTS.md from `assets/agents-block.md`. Fill `{{docs-path}}`, and `{{extra-never-write}}` with the spec-tool folders found, as `, openspec/ or .kiro/specs/`, else nothing. If the file has the markers, replace only what is between them. If it exists without markers, append the block at the end. If it doesn't exist, create it with a one-line title and the block. Everything outside the markers stays exactly as it was. The block is root-only; nested AGENTS.md files are named in the report, not written.\n8. Make sure the root CLAUDE.md imports AGENTS.md: add a line `@AGENTS.md` at the top of an existing one that lacks it, or create a CLAUDE.md holding only that line. Claude Code reads a CLAUDE.md anywhere above the repo in place of AGENTS.md, so the import is needed even when the repo had no CLAUDE.md of its own.\n9. Report what was written, the never-write folders, the nested AGENTS.md files the block doesn't cover, and the spec-tool folders left exactly where they were. Then ask the user to confirm the block loads: a new session asked \"where does project memory live?\" answers with `<docs-path>/index.md`.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `<docs-path>/index.md` has at least one domain row and `docs-format`. `stack.md` has a test command.\n- The root AGENTS.md holds the markers exactly once, and a diff shows no change outside them.\n- CLAUDE.md contains `@AGENTS.md`.\n- No `<!--` guidance, no \"None\" and no \"TBD\" in the two docs files.\n- On a rerun, only the text between the markers changed.\n\n## Fallbacks\n\n- No question tool: one plain-text message with the numbered questions and their recommended answers, then wait.\n- No way to start a session from here: give the user the one question to ask in a new session and the answer to expect.\n---\nname: docs-conventions\ndescription: The file charter and the claim format for a project's memory in docs/. Load before writing or changing any file under docs/, the index, glossary, behavior, architecture, decisions, specs, plans, tasks or reference files, and before editing the project-memory block in AGENTS.md. It says which file holds what, what each never contains, where misplaced content goes, and how a claim is written.\nuser-invocable: false\n---\n\n# Docs conventions\n\nProject memory lives under the docs path the project-memory block in AGENTS.md names, `docs/` unless the block says otherwise. Every file has one job. Content that matches a file's \"never contains\" column goes to the file the charter names for it.\n\n## Process\n\n1. Find the file's row in the charter. Read its reference under `references/` for the sections and their rules, and start from its template under `assets/`.\n2. Write only what the file's job covers. Move anything else to the file the row names.\n3. Remove every `<!-- -->` guidance comment. Delete an optional section that would be empty. Never write \"None\" or \"TBD\". Keep heading names and their order exactly as the template has them.\n4. Keep the frontmatter keys the template has, with no comments in the frontmatter. A quoted slot such as `id: \"{{slug}}\"` is filled without the quotes, unless the value needs them to stay valid YAML.\n5. Write claims in the claim format below, 3 to 7 per capability.\n\n## The charter\n\n| File | Job | Contains | Never contains, and where that goes | Template | Reference |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `index.md` | map of the product and its docs | what it is, the domains with their owners, where things are | details of any domain: `behavior/` | `assets/index.md` | `references/index.md` |\n| `glossary.md` | shared words | term, meaning, name in code, words to avoid | rules or behavior: `behavior/` | none, one line per term | `references/glossary.md` |\n| `behavior/<domain>/<capability>.md` | what the product does now | claims with IDs, one example each | how it's built: `architecture/`; history: git; plans: `specs/` | `assets/behavior-capability.md` | `references/behavior.md` |\n| `architecture/<area>.md` | how it's built now | one area per file, created when it first has content | behavior: `behavior/`; history: git; plans: `specs/` | `assets/architecture/` | `references/architecture.md` |\n| `decisions/<date>-<slug>.md` | why a lasting choice was made | context, options, decision, consequences | step-by-step detail: `plan.md` or `reference/` | `assets/decision.md` | `references/decision.md` |\n| `specs/<id>/spec.md` | why this change, and exactly what changes | why, claims under Add, Change and Remove, not doing, open questions | how it's built: `plan.md`; progress: `tasks.md` | `assets/spec.md` | `references/spec.md` |\n| `specs/<id>/plan.md` | how this change is built | approach, files to touch, architecture to update at ship, risks | the why: `spec.md`; progress: `tasks.md` | `assets/plan.md` | `references/plan.md` |\n| `specs/<id>/tasks.md` | where the work stands | session lines, tasks with the claims that prove them, short notes | design discussion: `plan.md` or `decisions/` | `assets/tasks.md` | `references/tasks.md` |\n| `specs/<id>/reference/*` | working material for this spec | anything the spec needs, opening with `Purpose: ...` | the why, the plan, progress: the three files above | none | `references/reference.md` |\n| AGENTS.md block | the workflow and where memory lives | the managed block between the markers | project knowledge: `docs/` | `assets/agents-block.md` | `references/agents-block.md` |\n\n## The claim format\n\n```\n- <Actor> <does or sees something observable, present tense> {#claim-id}\n  e.g. <a concrete situation> -> <the observable result>\n```\n\nOne testable sentence per claim, then one concrete example. IDs are kebab-case and unique within their capability. The full reference is `domain/capability#claim-id`; tests carry it in their names, and tasks use it whenever the bare ID is not unique in the spec. Observable behavior only: \"keeps one entry\", never \"uses a unique index\". A claim in `spec.md` reads exactly as it will in `behavior/`, so shipping copies it without rewording.\n\n## Versions\n\n`index.md` carries `docs-format`. A template change applies to new files only. Shipped specs and existing files are never rewritten to a newer format.\n\n## Verification\n\n- The file has the template's frontmatter keys and headings, in the template's order, and no `<!-- -->` comment anywhere.\n- No section is empty, and \"None\" and \"TBD\" appear nowhere.\n- Every claim has an ID and an `e.g.` line, and a capability has 3 to 7 of them.\n- Nothing in the file matches its \"never contains\" column.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
